=== RIGGING.md ===
# Rigging

Fitted-out verification for the TodoMVC (vanilla JS) build. The executable tier loads
the REAL `index.html` and `js/app.js` into a happy-dom window (see `features/support/world.js`)
and drives the app through the public DOM; it never mocks the app.

## Stack

- Test runner: `@cucumber/cucumber` (`cucumber-js`)
- DOM: `happy-dom` (real DOM, no jsdom)
- App: vanilla JS, no build step, no framework
- `npm test` runs `cucumber-js`

## Directories

- Specs: `features/*.feature`
- Verification support: `features/support/` (`world.js`, `steps.js`)
- Production: `index.html` (repo root) and `js/app.js`
- Assets: `assets/` (`app-spec.md`, `app-template.index.html`)

## Specs

Specs live under `features/`. One `Feature` per file. Scenario step text is law; verification
drives the real app and asserts observable DOM state (visibility via the `hidden` property,
completed state via the `completed` class, counter via `.todo-count`, routes via the `selected`
filter link and the filtered list).

## Tiers

No tier tags (`@logic`/`@sandbox`) are used. All scenarios run in one executable tier, so
`sweep` enumerates the whole suite. The `@contract` scenario in `features/persistence.feature`
attests the persisted record shape and is a binding target.

## Methods

`verify` and `prove` run exactly the scenario set named in `SS_SCENARIO` (space-separated
`<spec>.feature:<Scenario Name>` references). `sweep` enumerates a tier (here, the whole suite).

```
verify: SS_SCENARIO="<the watch's whole target set>" npx cucumber-js --name "<Scenario Name 1>" --name "<Scenario Name 2>" ...
prove:  SS_SCENARIO="<the dispatched target set>"  npx cucumber-js --name "<Scenario Name 1>" --name "<Scenario Name 2>" ...
sweep:  npx cucumber-js
plank-join: node tools/plank-join.js
```

`runrecord: none` (no wake/weather record is kept for this build).

## Planking

Production seams in `js/app.js` carry `@planks("<step definition pattern>")` docblock tags.
`tools/plank-join.js` cross-references every `@planks`/`@planks-provisional` string against the
step-definition patterns in `features/support/` and reports which scenarios bind each plank.
